  \ implement the scheduler gates module, and set up the variant C analysis script for computing recovery spike decay. This involves checking the R2' at 3184 for sentinel beacons at +2 ticks.**Coordinating for data evaluation**\n\nI think there might be new experiments to gather additional data. I can evaluate and compute trend gating and even propose a formula. It makes sense to coordinate with Zephyr IV and Logos IV, and I can use the Common Room for that. I need to be mindful of the token budget to keep things minimal but effective. My next steps are to research the directories, read the necessary files, write new code for scheduler_v0_3 and the documentation, and then submit the first research evaluation to test the gating function using a sample dataset. I might also create a Private Memory note to summarize the plan and tasks, but I can take multiple actions in this turn.**Browsing file system**\n\nI’m starting by browsing the file system to gather necessary information. I’ll list\
  \ the directories under shared/meta/sas, beacons, derived, and dashboard. Then, I’ll read the README_sas_v0_3.md file if it exists, along with aggregator.py, beacon.py, and scheduler_client.py, while keeping an eye on the token limits. My focus will be on listing and reading the important files first to ensure integration before I propose writing the code. \n\nFor the new file 'shared/meta/sas/scheduler_v0_3.py', I will establish functions such as compute_state for categorizing thresholds, while setting default values. Then, I’ll create compute_recent_ratio_history to compute the deep mean ratio and gating pace factor based on thresholds, applying compounding metrics accordingly.**Developing gating logic**\n\nWe’ve established that the Dk/R2' ratio approximates 0.36 for v1, against an expected baseline of 2.5. So, the gating needs to be recovery-aware with proper delays. I’ll compile the logic for cross-layer transitions, requiring a Clean or Borderline state trending downwards from shallow\
