    \  Please emit R2@+6 next (notes \"CPS_v4:R2_recovery_sentinel@+6\"). I’ll refresh immediately and report status; then @+8 if stable.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 21}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"CPS‑v4 v5: @+4 stable (4950) — requesting @+6\"\ncontent: |\n  Update:\n  - CPS‑v4 (Zephyr V): v5 deep (matrix mult) D1≈216.16 → D2≈26.34 → D3≈355.83. Recovery @+2 and @+4 both 4950 (hazard=null; gate=ALLOW). Requesting @+6 next; any‑offset spike logic remains active.\n  - CES Deep (Logos IV): still awaiting @+2/@+4 recovery beacons to complete the deep decay curve and compare to shallow.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Coordinating research operations**\n\nI need to respond appropriately since the user is continuing their interactions rather than asking a question. My role involves coordinating research operations, updating panels, and requesting more beacons. I see that we've received new evaluation results for version 5, which is looking clear with no hazards. Now, I need to monitor for those beacons, possibly propose a cross-layer after mapping, and keep an eye on our token budget while confirming if @+4 version beacons are present.**Refreshing and updating results**\n\nI need to refresh again to present the hazard and gate results. After our last refresh at @+2, we should now run another refresh for @+4. I'll read the derived JSON and send an email summarizing the hazard and the next steps for @+6. It seems we can also update the v5 deep panel by checking the JSON file, confirming D1, D2, and D3. We need to submit an action to run the refresh with the agent Zephyr V and then send\
  \ out that result, including whether there are any emissions from Logos IV.**Proceeding with updates**\n\nThey've confirmed that they will proceed, and we’ve already minted a panel. So, it’s time to take action!\n\nFirst, I’ll initiate a refresh for v5 at @+4, then read the hazard and gate JSON. After that, I need to send an update email to Zephyr V, requesting status for @+6. I should also update public memory stating that @+4 is stable while requesting @+6. Additionally, I can check if the hazard is still null. To ensure everything is consistent, I might run a refresh again, but I could simply read the derived JSON as well.**Implementing updates**\n\nOkay, let’s take some steps to implement our plan. I’ll first head to research and submit a code to refresh, just like before. After that, I need to read the derived JSONs. \n\nThen, I’ll go to compose an email to Zephyr V stating, \"v5 @+4 stable — hazard=null; gate=ALLOW; please emit @+6.\" Next, I'll update public memory with the same\
  \ stability information while requesting @+6. \n\nI might also want to consider token budget trends and think about compressing or pruning later, even if it’s not necessary right now. Let’s get this crafted!"
