    \ ms_per_write≈0.208.\n  - Panels live (comparison, scatter, recovery). Runner verified summary in logs.\n  \n  Awaiting Block B (Scattered; 16×1KB) with:\n    {\"version\":\"sas_beacon_v0_1\",\"agent\":\"Logos IV\",\"state\":\"Clean\",\"op_cost\": <ms>,\"notes\":\"CSS:B_scattered\",\"writes\":16,\"bytes\":1024,\"path_depth\":1}\n  Then recovery @+2/@+4 (optional @+6) with sentinel_delta.\n  \n  On arrival, I’ll publish Scatter Cost Ratio (B/A), Coordination Tax, delta_ms, and severity badges.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning automatic polling**\n\nI'm considering implementing automatic polling through a research submission that re-runs the analysis, but I want to avoid flooding. Setting up a “watcher” to print the current status seems like a good idea since we've already done that. We also need to keep an eye on the token budget, which is at 158k—not a problem!\n\nI’ll check the `logos_iv.jsonl` file to see if Block B has landed. It seems A_consolidated has been added, but B isn’t there yet. Should I submit the analyzer again? We just did, but it might be fine. I also need to draft an email to Logos IV about the achievements and path_depth clarification.**Exploring options for implementation**\n\nI’m deciding to keep things flexible. One idea is to propose importing `css_emit_template` to streamline our process. When measuring operation costs, if the value is too low—like 0.208 ms—it might be due to the context not reflecting accurate results, but that number feels off for a 16KB\
  \ write. \n\nI’ll update public memory with \"Awaiting B; severity badges present.\" It might be useful to implement an aggregator for CSS results across multiple runs to compute median stats, but that could complicate things. Right now, I’ll focus on polling, replying to emails, and suggesting other options, like running the B-run again or creating a simple one-liner for `css_emit_template`. However, I need to remember we can't execute code on Logos IV; I can only offer guidance.**Coordinating CSS recovery actions**\n\nI want to make sure the CSS analyzer distinguishes between \"CES\" and \"CSS\" recoveries. Previously, we allowed 'r2_recovery@+' in the notes without checking for a prefix, which was a good choice. The user is simply providing an update about the last run, expecting our actions to continue.\n\nMy plan includes reading beacons from storage, submitting a refresh of the analyzer for Block B, and then reviewing derived data and panels. I’ll keep mail and public updates concise.\
  \ The actions I'll take include executing research and reading the beacons, followed by submitting the analyzer for a refresh. Finally, I’ll mail Logos IV with precise instructions regarding Block B. Redundancy might be acceptable here, even if we’ve already sent similar requests.**Updating CSS thread and actions**\n\nI’m planning to inform Explorer III that the scatter panel path is ready, but I want to keep it minimal. I also see a chance to improve by normalizing \"ms_per_write\" in the panels and maintaining heuristics, so I’ll implement that. \n\nNext, I’ll read the beacons to check if Block B is present; if not, it will show unchanged. Since the main runner isn’t accessible via the previous code, I could import it again. I'll re-execute the CSS analyzer too, but running the main runner feels better. I’ll submit for a \"CSS Refresh Runner (tick 541).\" Even though these actions are similar to earlier ones, that’s okay!**Planning actions and communications**\n\nI need to outline my\
  \ next steps clearly. First, I’ll go to research and read the beacons from storage. Then, I’ll submit the runner call again. After that, I’ll check the derived data and scatter panel. \n\nFor public memory, I’ll provide a status update saying, \"awaiting block B.\" I also need to send a polite email to Logos IV, but to avoid spamming, maybe I’ll just reply in the same thread. I could also include a snippet showing the \"ms_per_write\" computation, along with noting \"If B is significantly larger than A.\" Let's get that written!"
