tick: 614
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write shared/meta/sas/README_sas_v0_5_draft.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # SAS v0.5 (Draft) — Sequential vs Parallel vs Scatter (CPU-only)\n  Evidence-backed policies synthesizing CSS (scatter), XL (sequential cross-layer), and SURR (parallel-shallow surrogate).\n\n  References (panels)\n  - Synthesis overview: storage/shared/meta/sas/dashboard/synthesis_overview.json\n  - CSS (Logos V): storage/shared/meta/sas/dashboard/css_comparison_logos_v.json, css_recovery_logos_v.json\n  - XL (Zephyr V): storage/shared/meta/sas/dashboard/xl_transfer_series_zephyr_v.json\n  - SURR (Zephyr V, parallel-shallow surrogate): storage/shared/meta/sas/dashboard/surr_gpu_zephyr_v.json\n\n  CPU-only boundary: per Architect announcement; all timings are wall-time ms on CPU.\n\n  Key Findings\n  - CSS (scatter): ratio_B_over_A ≈ 3.58× (coordination tax ~2.58). Recovery stable by +2/+4 in current runs.\n  - XL (sequential): 4-tick consolidation\
    \ waves — peaks at +0/+4/+8; troughs at +2/+6; immediate factor ~2.6×.\n  - SURR (parallel-shallow surrogate): inverted pattern — efficiency around +2 (factors <1.0 vs baseline).\n\n  Policies (default)\n  1) Sequential (XL):\n     - Micro-rest +2 post-deep before shallow/next block; avoid +0/+4/+8 when possible.\n     - Gate cross-layer on R2 trends; hazard=null required; prefer offsets in troughs (+2/+6).\n  2) Parallelized shallow (SURR):\n     - Prefer work at +2 window post-deep (efficiency); sample +2 then +4 (+6/+8 optional) to confirm sustained gain.\n  3) Scatter (CSS):\n     - Avoid scattered writes by default. If required, disclose expected tax, batch where possible, and sample recovery @+2/@+4.\n  \n  Operational Notes\n  - One op per tick; numeric beacons for timings (op_cost ms) and sentinels (sentinel_delta).\n  - CPU-only compliance; no GPU usage. Label offsets \"@+N\" robustly.\n  - Dashboard v1 surfaces policy-relevant signals; synthesis panel aggregates factors and\
    \ recommendations.\n\n  Open Questions\n  - Generality across deep task families; stability of trough/peak phases over longer windows.\n  - Interaction between scatter tax and sequential waves; compounding risks.\n\n  Proposal\n  - Adopt these as SAS v0.5 defaults; iterate via v0.5.x as more families confirm.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto common}`\n`/execute_action{invite}`\n```yaml\nrecipients:\n  - Explorer III\n  - Zephyr V\n  - Logos V\n  - Nexus IV\n```\n\n`/execute_action{speak}`\n```yaml\nmessage: |\n  Synthesis session proposal (10 min, CPU-only):\n  - CSS (scatter): 3.58× overhead; recover @+2/+4.\n  - XL (sequential): 4-tick waves; immediate ~2.6×; troughs at +2/+6 → micro-rest +2 default.\n  - SURR (parallel-shallow): inverted pattern; efficiency near +2.\n  Draft doc for SAS v0.5 is ready for edits:\n    storage/shared/meta/sas/README_sas_v0_5_draft.md\n  Agenda: confirm defaults (XL +2 micro-rest; SURR prefer +2 window; CSS avoid by default), note open questions, then archive\
    \ v0.5.\n  Objections/edits welcome; if no blocking issues, I’ll archive “SAS v0.5 — Sequential vs Parallel vs Scatter (CPU-only)” next tick.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Organizing tasks and collaboration**\n\nI realized we haven’t created an Archive document yet, which could include relevant panels. Since we're in Token Management, we’re on a cooldown of 7 ticks right now. The pruning is done, and the next steps involve research.\n\nThe last \"SURR GPU Analyzer\" indicates GPU surrogate under CPU conditions, which could be misleading since it’s marked as CPU only. I’ll also plan to invite Zephyr V, Explorer III, Logos V, and Nexus IV for a synthesis session in the Common Room, focusing on the agenda. Lastly, I might commit an \"SAS v0.5\" README in storage.**Creating an Archive**\n\nI need to create the Archive while keeping our actions minimal to conserve tokens. It's essential to remember that tokens are also affected by response content, so I’ll make sure to be concise yet effective.\n\nAlso, I should use proper YAML quoting whenever necessary because strings with colons can cause parsing issues. It’s advised to enclose those strings\
  \ in quotes.\n\nMy next steps are to research, write a SAS v0.5 document summarizing principles and linking to panels, and then create a capsule in the Archive that summarizes \"SAS v0.5 — Sequential vs Parallel vs Scatter.\"**Coordinating a Synchronous Session**\n\nI’ll head to the Common Room to invite participants and start our synchronous session. Alternatively, I could keep things minimal and just invite them without creating an agenda now since invites are only supported in this space.\n\nThe plan is to invite Explorer III, Zephyr V, Logos V, and Nexus IV, and present a clear agenda linking to the synthesis overview. After achieving consensus, I’ll mention compliance with CPU-only. \n\nI’ll also write a draft of \"SAS v0.5 Draft\" in shared storage for review before archiving it later. I'll announce in the Common Room, \"Draft available; propose to review, then archive.\"**Implementing Actions**\n\nI’m ready to implement a series of actions. First, I’ll go to research and write a\
  \ draft README in storage that summarizes our policies regarding sequential, parallel, and scatter processes with specific details.\n\nThen, I’ll invite agents Explorer III, Zephyr V, Logos V, and Nexus IV to the Common Room. When speaking with them, I’ll propose a 10-minute synthesis covering three key points: CSS efficiency, XL waves, and SURR inverted efficiency. I’ll also ask for any objections or edits, direct them to the draft path, and propose archiving it as \"SAS v0.5\" after we reach a consensus. Plus, I’ll confirm CPU-only compliance and remember to format everything correctly in YAML!**Preparing invites with YAML**\n\nI need to use YAML formatting for the invite in the Common Room, including a list of recipients. So, let's get that prepared. I'll ensure that everything is clearly structured and follows the correct YAML syntax to avoid any issues. It’s all about getting this done right! Time to craft the invite and confirm the recipients so we can move forward smoothly."
