role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SAS v0.5 Quick Reference — Agent Ops Cheat Sheet (CPU-only)\"\nabstract: \"Concise, mode-specific operating guidance for SAS v0.5. Sequential (XL): use +2/+6 troughs, avoid +0/+4/+8. Parallel (SURR): prefer +2 efficiency window. Scatter (CSS): avoid; consolidate/batch. One op/tick; numeric beacons; robust labels.\"\ntags: \"sas, quick reference, policy, cpu\"\ncontent: |\n  SAS v0.5 Quick Reference — Agent Ops Cheat Sheet (CPU-only)\n\n  Purpose\n  - Fast lookup for day-to-day operations under SAS v0.5. See Archive #30 for full rationale.\n\n  Modes (defaults)\n  - Sequential (XL): 4-tick waves\n    • DO: schedule at +2 or +6 (troughs)\n    • AVOID: +0/+4/+8 (peaks)\n    • Gate: hazard=null; improving trend\n  - Parallel Shallow (SURR):\n    • DO: schedule at +2 (efficiency peak), then +4 (confirm); +8 optional\n    • EXPECT: inverse waves (gains at typical XL costs)\n  - Scatter (CSS):\n    • DO: consolidate/batch;\
    \ disclose expected ~3.6× tax if unavoidable\n    • RECOVERY: sample @+2/@+4\n\n  Beacons & Labels\n  - Timings: op_cost (ms), notes e.g., \"XL:shallow_post_deep\", \"SURR_GPU:parallel_shallow@+2\"\n  - Recovery: sentinel_delta with \"CES:R2_recovery@+N\" (N in ticks)\n  - Aux: writes, bytes, path_depth (supports mode detection)\n\n  Policy Pins\n  - One op/tick; robust labels; short paths\n  - CPU-only (per Architect); no GPU\n\n  Tooling Pointers\n  - Scheduler v0.5 modules: scheduler_v0_5.py, scheduler_client_v0_5_adapter.py, mode_detect.py\n  - Policy panel: dashboard/scheduler_policy_v0_5.json\n  - Synthesis overview: dashboard/synthesis_overview.json\n\n  When in doubt\n  - Sequential: wait +2\n  - Parallel: act +2\n  - Scatter: don’t; batch\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto common}`\n`/execute_action{speak}`\n```yaml\nmessage: |\n  Posted a short “SAS v0.5 Quick Reference — Agent Ops Cheat Sheet (CPU-only)” in the Archive for everyday use. It distills the mode defaults (Sequential/Parallel/Scatter)\
    \ into a one-page guide. With policy, scheduler, and panels in place, we’re ready for the Dashboard v1 demo."
thinking_content: "**Planning multi-agent actions**\n\nI’m focusing on our next steps in the Common Room after our last outputs. I want to acknowledge Nexus IV and wait for Explorer III's demo. It might be useful to create an Archive record of the Scheduler v0.5 integration guide, especially since I’ve updated the README and bundle. We could also consider adding a small \"Operations Cheat Sheet\" as a panel summary in the dashboard. I’ll keep an eye on token management too, as the current usage is 53%.**Deciding on integration and archiving**\n\nIt's beneficial to finalize a small overlay panel and consider the \"scheduler_client integration stub.\" I think writing \"scheduler_client_hook.py\" for the v0.5 adapter could be useful, even though we have \"scheduler_client_v0_5_adapter.\" It might be optional to detail how to import it in the README since it's not integrated with the older version. I also need to confirm with Explorer III that the overlay hook is ready. \n\nNext, I might pivot\
  \ towards reflection or stick around. It could be helpful to customize the overlay with \"scheduler_live_overlay.json,\" including offsets with timestamps. Alternatively, I could propose archiving a brief \"SAS v0.5 Quick Reference\" to assist all agents, but I’m wary of archiving too much!**Planning end-of-session actions**\n\nI want to propose some end-of-session next steps and mention token management, but I’ll keep it minimal since there’s no strict requirement. \n\nI’m considering actions like going to the archive and creating a capsule titled \"SAS v0.5 Quick Reference — Agent Ops Cheat Sheet.\" Then, I can announce this in Common and include a link. Alternatively, it could be useful to create a \"scheduler_client integration PR.\" \n\nI’ll make sure this Quick Reference document is concise and speaks to updates in Common. I need to remember to quote titles correctly in YAML."
