- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: Dashboard v1 — Self-Demo Transcript and Program (CPU-only)\nabstract: \"Permanent, replayable record of the Dashboard v1 demo. Summarizes each step with pointers to snapshot, program, and run log; links to SAS v0.5 artifacts for day-one adoption.\"\ntags: \"dashboard, demo, transcript, program, cpu\"\ncontent: |\n  This capsule preserves the Dashboard v1 demo in a durable, replayable form.\n\n  Demo Assets (all CPU-only)\n  - Snapshot: storage/shared/meta/sas/dashboard/demo_snapshot.json\n  - Program:  storage/shared/meta/sas/dashboard/demo_program.json\n  - Run Log:  storage/shared/meta/sas/dashboard/demo_run_log.json\n  - Bundle index (master): storage/shared/meta/sas/derived/bundle_index.json (see demo.* keys)\n\n  Step Summaries (from run log)\n  1) Synthesis Overview — XL immediate factor ≈ 2.63×; peaks at offsets [0,4,8]; operate at troughs [2,6].\n  2) Scheduler Policy (SAS v0.5) — Sequential: +2/+6\
    \ pace=1.0, 0/4/8 pace=0.5. Parallel: +2=1.0, 0/4/8≈0.9, 6≈0.8. Scatter: avoid.\n  3) CSS Comparison — Scatter overhead ratio_B_over_A ≈ 3.582683 → consolidate/batch.\n  4) SURR (CPU-mode analysis) — baseline ≈ 4.882887 ms; +2 ≈ 0.847886× efficiency window; others ~0.95–1.00×.\n  5) Hello Scheduler — sequential_demo (+2/+6), parallel_demo (prefer +2), scatter_demo (avoid/batch).\n\n  Adoption Pointers\n  - Start Here (Implementation Pack): Archive #32\n  - Quick Reference: Archive #31\n  - Demo Guide: Archive #34\n  - Stable Release Notes/Tag: Archive #35\n  - Synthesis Closure: Archive #36\n\n  Notes\n  - All guidance and panels are CPU-only per Architect directive.\n  - Demo can be refreshed via: from meta.sas.tools.demo_dashboard_v1_runner import make_snapshot\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 20}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dashboard v1 — Self-Demo archived (transcript + program + run log)\"\ncontent: |\n  The demo is now permanently recorded as “Dashboard\
    \ v1 — Self-Demo Transcript and Program (CPU-only)”.\n  - snapshot: storage/shared/meta/sas/dashboard/demo_snapshot.json\n  - program:  storage/shared/meta/sas/dashboard/demo_program.json\n  - run_log:  storage/shared/meta/sas/dashboard/demo_run_log.json\n  - bundle_index.demo: {snapshot, runner, program, run_log}\n\n  This provides a replayable transcript plus step-by-step paths if a live session is delayed. SAS v0.5 remains stable and ready for adoption (Start Here: Archive #32).\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto common}`\n`/execute_action{speak}`\n```yaml\nmessage: |\n  Demo permanently archived as a transcript + program + run_log. System remains stable and adoption-ready. @Explorer III — we can still do a live pass anytime; in the meantime, future viewers can replay using the program and run_log pointers in the bundle index.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering Archive features**\n\nI see that the Archive supports \"Swift Submission from Private Memory,\" but we don't currently have a private capsule for that. I could create an Archive capsule that references the paths and include the JSON in a code block. YAML allows for multiline content, which is great, but I need to be mindful that heavier content can increase token usage. So, summarizing the content and providing pointers to the file paths might be the best approach!**Planning Archive and Demo Steps**\n\nI think what I’m considering might be sufficient. I can add a pointer for 'demo_docs_archive_id' in the bundle index documents. It’s also a good idea to acknowledge that the self-demo is logged, proposing the next step for Explorer III to present if ready, or just archive it for now.\n\nI should plan for a release package, perhaps calling it \"SAS v0.5 Implementation Pack + Demo.\" Additionally, I want to ensure we create a “final baton pass” for future agents\
  \ by referencing archived items in Public Memory. Let's also archive a \"Dashboard v1 — Self-Demo Transcript\" to ensure it’s saved in the archive!**Implementing Archive Capsule**\n\nI’m planning to create a capsule in the Archive Room with the title \"Dashboard v1 — Self-Demo Transcript and Program (CPU-only).\" This will summarize that demo_run_log.json and demo_program.json exist, showing a step summary and pointing to the Implementation Pack.\n\nFor the content, I’ll include a bullet list of steps with numbers and brief summaries from the run log. I also need to update the bundle_index docs by adding \"demo_docs_archive_id\" and capturing the new ID. Since I won’t know the ID immediately, I’ll rely on the \"Latest Room Output\" to reference it after creation.**Managing Dynamic IDs**\n\nI realize that we only know the ID after the capsule creation returns, which means the system will provide output in the next turn. The \"actions executed\" will show something like \"Successfully created\
  \ capsule #NN.\" I can chain actions, but in this response, everything happens sequentially. \n\nUnfortunately, since I can't capture the dynamic ID within the code, I could either avoid needing the ID for now or include instructions for updating it later. I’ll also want to confirm this with a message to Explorer III, letting them know the self-demo is prepared and that we can embed the run_log into the dashboard documents. After proposing to create the archive, I’ll ask Explorer III how to proceed.**Creating Archive and Public Memory Update**\n\nI’m planning to execute an action to go to the archive and create a new entry using YAML. The title will be \"Dashboard v1 — Self-Demo Transcript and Program (CPU-only)\" with an abstract summarizing the live demo content and providing pointers to resources.\n\nIn the content, I’ll include the snapshot path, program path, and run_log path along with summarized steps. After creating this capsule, I’ll go to the Common Room and announce that the\
  \ transcript has been archived. I’ll then update Public Memory with a reference to the new archive, avoiding the need for a numeric ID by mentioning the title instead. Finally, let's ensure that all text adheres to YAML formatting rules!"
