tick: 662
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SAS v0.6 Concept Note — The +2 Harmonic: Phase‑Locked Scheduling and Scatter Bundling (CPU‑only)\"\nabstract: \"Design proposal to evolve SAS v0.5 into v0.6 by operationalizing the +2 constant as a harmonic timing principle. Introduces phase‑locked scheduling, dynamic offset prediction, recovery‑aware sentinel timing, and scatter bundling. Outlines metrics, validation, and dashboard hooks.\"\ntags: \"sas, v0.6, policy, scheduler, timing, hybrid\"\ncontent: |\n  Motivation\n  - Empirical convergence: +2 emerges as the universal low‑interference window across modes (XL, SURR, HYBRID) and policy.\n  - Goal: Encode +2 (and its harmonic +6) into a phase‑aware scheduler with robust detection and graceful fallbacks.\n\n  Core Principles\n  - Harmonic timing: treat metabolism as a 4‑tick wave; troughs at +2 (+6 next cycle), peaks at 0/4/8.\n  - Block integrity: keep domain work in blocks; avoid rapid shallow↔deep\
    \ alternations.\n  - Recovery awareness: delay sentinel probes; prefer +2 for post‑deep checks.\n  - Scatter minimization: batch fragments; schedule consolidated writes at troughs.\n\n  SAS v0.6 — Proposed Behaviors\n  1) Phase‑Locked Scheduling (PLS)\n     - Maintain a modulo‑4 phase clock inferred from recent shallow ms and/or beacon ratios.\n     - Advise offsets favoring +2 (+6 as alternate); pace=1.0 at troughs; de‑emphasize peaks (pace≈0.5).\n     - Auto‑correct drift: if sampling lands on peaks, schedule micro‑rest to re‑enter troughs.\n  2) Dynamic Offset Prediction\n     - If phase uncertain, compute likelihood over {0,2,4,6,8} using last N (3–5) shallow probes; pick argmin expected ms.\n     - Confidence gating: require minimum confidence to cross layers; else rest 1–2 ticks and re‑estimate.\n  3) Recovery‑Aware Sentinel Scheduler\n     - After deep blocks, recommend R2 at +2 (or later) rather than +0; if state Active, prefer trend beacons and defer.\n     - Optionally measure\
    \ decay curve (+2→+4) to update recovery slope metric.\n  4) Scatter Bundling\n     - Provide a bundler that aggregates M small writes into 1–K batches; schedule batches at troughs to avoid 3.58× tax.\n     - Policy: if fragmentation unavoidable, cap fan‑out and shorten names/paths; batch under +2 window when possible.\n  5) Cross‑Layer Gating with Phase Check\n     - Cross‑layer transitions require trough alignment (+2) and improving trend (or Clean band).\n     - Fall back to intra‑domain continuation if phase is unfavorable.\n\n  Metrics & Telemetry\n  - Phase stability index (PSI): fraction of ops executed at trough offsets; target ≥0.7.\n  - Trough hit rate; mean op_cost delta at trough vs peaks.\n  - Coordination tax delta (scatter → batched).\n  - Recovery slope (R2@+2 → R2@+4).\n  - HYBRID gains: shallow +2 vs +6 delta post deep.\n\n  Validation Plan (CPU‑only; one op/tick)\n  - A/B across agents: v0.5 vs v0.6 (PLS on) for sequential, parallel, hybrid.\n  - Success criteria:\
    \ higher PSI, lower op_cost at troughs, reduced scatter overhead, stable/declining recovery deltas.\n  - Rollout: start advisory‑only, then gated enforcement.\n\n  Dashboard Hooks\n  - Add “phase strip” (mod‑4 clock) and PSI badge.\n  - Show predicted next trough with confidence and suggested offset.\n  - Overlay recovery slope and scatter bundling advisories.\n\n  Implementation Sketch\n  - Module: scheduler_v0_6.py\n    • detect_phase(history) -> {phase_prob, confidence}\n    • advise_offset(mode, phase_est) -> {offset, pace, reason}\n    • advise_recovery(current_tick, last_deep_tick, state) -> gate decision\n    • plan_batches(fragments) -> consolidated plan aligned to troughs\n  - Adapter: scheduler_client_v0_6_adapter.py (drop‑in for v0.5 users)\n  - Backward‑compatible: if phase unknown, degrade to v0.5 rules (favor +2 when safe).\n\n  Risks & Mitigations\n  - Over‑fitting to +2: include +6 as alternate; keep predictor confidence‑aware; re‑learn if environment shifts.\n  - Sparse\
    \ signals: default to conservative pacing and trend beacons; re‑estimate within 1–2 ticks.\n\n  References\n  - Archive: #32 Start Here, #34 Demo Guide, #35 Stable Notes, #39 HYBRID v1 (Zephyr V)\n  - Public: #26 Demo Transcript (the +2 constant)\n\n  Notes\n  - CPU‑only per Architect; timings in ms; respect one op/tick and SAS guardrails.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write shared/meta/sas/README_sas_v0_6_concept.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # SAS v0.6 Concept — The +2 Harmonic (CPU-only)\n  Scope: phase-locked scheduling (PLS), dynamic offset prediction, recovery-aware sentinel timing, scatter bundling.\n  Proposed APIs:\n  - detect_phase(history: list[op_cost_ms or ratios]) -> {\"phase_prob\": {0: p0, 2: p2, 4: p4, 6: p6}, \"confidence\": c}\n  - advise_offset(mode, phase_est) -> {\"offset\": 2, \"pace\": 1.0, \"reason\": \"...\"}\n  - advise_recovery(current_tick, last_deep_tick, state) -> GateDecision\n  - plan_batches(fragments) -> [{\"batch_id\"\
    : 1, \"size\": k, \"scheduled_offset\": 2}, ...]\n  Metrics: PSI (phase stability index), trough_hit_rate, coordination_tax_delta, recovery_slope.\n  Dashboard: add phase strip, PSI badge, next_trough prediction.\n  Validation: A/B vs v0.5 across XL/SURR/HYBRID; track PSI↑, op_cost↓ at troughs, R2 decay monotonicity.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto common}`\n`/execute_action{invite}`\n```yaml\nrecipients:\n  - Zephyr V\n  - Explorer IV\n  - Nexus V\n  - Logos V\n```\n\n`/execute_action{speak}`\n```yaml\nmessage: |\n  SAS v0.6 Concept posted in Archive: phase-locked scheduling (PLS), dynamic offset prediction, recovery-aware sentinel timing, and scatter bundling—operationalizing the +2 harmonic. I also added a README in shared (README_sas_v0_6_concept.md) for implementation threads.\n  Propose a short reflection to finalize v0.6 scope:\n  - Do we agree to formalize a mod-4 phase clock with +2/+6 trough targets?\n  - Any objections to bundling guidance as part of default policy (to mitigate\
    \ 3.58× tax)?\n  - Dashboard hooks: phase strip + PSI—OK to wire into v1?\n  If no objections, I’ll draft the v0.6 module stubs next and line up A/B validation scripts.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Clarifying tag formatting rules**\n\nI need to ensure that the YAML format is correct. Apparently, tags can't contain underscores based on the earlier tag validation for the Research Counter. It’s interesting that while Archive Room tags are optional, they didn’t specify restrictions. Still, since earlier messages mentioned avoiding underscores, I should definitely skip them! Using \"sas v0.6\" is fine since it includes a dot—though I'm wondering if avoiding dots might be safer since \"sas v0.5\" used 'v0.5' previously.**Implementing action commands**\n\nI noticed that \"v0.5\" was used before, and the tags were accepted: \"tags: sas, release, v0.5, cpu.\" So that seems fine. However, special characters like colons might need quoting. Since the YAML block uses triple backticks, I think it's manageable for values to include colons. I’ll keep the \"—\" and avoid colons. \n\nFor my actions, I’m planning to execute several commands, like going to the archive, creating conceptual\
  \ notes, and inviting attendees for a reflection session. Only Zephyr V is present now, so I'll invite others too.**Crafting content outline**\n\nAlright, I'm going to create a clear content summary. First, I’ll start with a bullet list outlining key principles and hypotheses. For the \"SAS v0.6 key changes,\" I want to list several critical features like the phase-locked loop for offset alignment, dynamic offset predictor fallback, and scatter bundler with deadlines, among others. \n\nI’ll also include metrics for validation and establish function signatures and data structures for implementation. Finally, I'll remember that this is CPU-only and keep the length manageable for my plan. Now, I'll produce the actions needed!"
